      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "This problem can be solved using the binomial probability formula.\n1. Consider the probability of rolling an even number on each die. There are 6 even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12) out of 12 possible outcomes, so the probability is $\\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. We want to calculate the probability of rolling exactly two even numbers. To do this, we can think of selecting 2 dice out of 5 to roll even numbers and the rest to roll odd numbers. So we can split this probability into three parts: choosing which 2 dice will roll even numbers (which can be done in $\\binom{5}{2}$ ways), and then calculating the probability that the other 3 dice will all roll odd numbers (which is $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3$).\n3. Using the binomial probability formula, this comes out to $\\binom{5}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\binom{5}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^6 = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} \\cdot \\frac{1}{64} = \\frac{10}{64} = \\frac{5}{32}$.\nSo, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is $\\boxed{\\frac{5}{32}}$.",
